# Exam track — Theme: Describing a Picture — Sub-lesson 1: General Description
#
# The second picture theme, and the counterpart to Schueberfouer: where that
# photo is a summer funfair, this one is a WINTER city street — the Groussgaass
# (Grand-Rue) pedestrian zone in Luxembourg City. The contrast is deliberate:
# the seasonal inference in 03 lands on the opposite answer, so the hedging
# frames get exercised rather than memorised.
#
# The photo: a cobbled pedestrian street seen down its length, tall buildings
# with shops and cafés left and right, a woman in a white jacket and red scarf
# walking in the foreground, a child in pink further back in the middle, a
# construction hoarding on the left, bare trees and cars far away at the end.
#
# The photo's coverage is split across three sub-lessons, one per exam task:
#   01 — WHERE + WHAT: naming the place, the objects and their positions, and
#        what the people are doing.                    ← this file
#   02 — WHO:  the people, 1–2 of them described visually.
#   03 — WHEN: the weather, and a hedged guess at the season and time of day.
#
# PURE DESCRIPTION. No @question examiner prompts and no first-person opinion,
# preference or feeling — those belong to the topic themes. A depicted person's
# visible state is fine: that is observation. Rule of thumb — if a stranger
# could not check the sentence against the photo, it does not belong here.
#
# Not authored, for failing exactly that test: the shop *names* legible on the
# signs. A brand name is neither Luxembourgish vocabulary nor checkable
# description, which is why the source description's `e Chantier, dee seet dass
# Ladurée gebaut gëtt` ships here as the plain `Lénks ass e Chantier.`
#
# Vocabulary and grammar are held to A1–A2 deliberately, same as the other
# picture theme: present tense, simple main clauses, spatial adverbs +
# prepositions, no attributive adjectives on masculine or neuter nouns.
#
# This file NAMES THE PLACE, which is the examiner's first question and the gap
# a scene-elements-only file leaves: d'Foussgängerzon, d'Groussgaass, de Zentrum.
# LOD lists Groussgaass as the Luxembourgish name of the Grand-Rue, so it is
# safe as a named place.
#
# Nouns are taught in the number the content actually uses (d'Butteker, d'Beem,
# d'Autoen as plurals) — an unused singular is a free Element standing between
# the learner and the 100% gate.
#
# The @fill blocks take frames from the library in
# .claude/memory/picture-description-theme.md that the Schueberfouer theme left
# unused: the place-noun frame, the two-coordinated-nouns inventory, the
# additive `Ausserdeem` listing, and the background inventory. See the skill's
# references/content-contract.md for the mechanized bounds.

@lesson P2.01 "Groussgaass: General Description"

# Pre-cropped to 16:9 and encoded as WebP at 880px — see
# .claude/memory/picture-description-theme.md for the pipeline. @image-alt stays:
# it is the a11y text, and the caption of the placeholder frame if the file is
# ever missing.
@image "/assets/exam/picture/groussgaass/img/groussgaass.webp"
@image-alt "A cobbled pedestrian shopping street in a city on a grey winter day: tall buildings with shops on the left and right, a woman in a white jacket and red scarf walking in the foreground, a child in pink further back, a construction hoarding on the left, bare trees at the far end."

# --- Talking about a picture ---
@word d'Bild = the picture
@word gesinn = to see

# --- Naming the place ---
@word d'Stad = the city
@word d'Strooss = the street
@word d'Foussgängerzon = the pedestrian zone
@word d'Groussgaass = the Grand-Rue
@word de Zentrum = the centre

# --- What is in the street ---
@word d'Gebaier = the buildings
@word d'Butteker = the shops
@word d'Caféen = the pubs
@word de Chantier = the construction site
@word d'Stroosseluuchten = the street lamps
@word d'Autoen = the cars
@word d'Beem = the trees
@word héich = tall

# --- Positions ---
@word lénks = on the left
@word riets = on the right
@word hannen = at the back

# --- People and what they do ---
@word d'Leit = the people
@word akafen = to do the shopping
@word ausserdeem = besides
@word mee = but
@word wann = when
@word richteg = correct
@word wéi = as
@word de Grupp = the group
@word munch = many a
@word d'Enn = the end
@word wierklech = really
@word d'Vitrinn = the shop window
@word de Client = the customer
@word d'Persoun = the person
@word lieweg = lively
@word stoen = to stand
@word datt = that
@word falsch = false

@sentence
@lu Dëst ass e Bild vun der Groussgaass.
@en This is a picture of the Grand-Rue.
@distractor-lu Duerf
@distractor-lu Bësch
@distractor-en village
@distractor-en forest

@sentence
@lu Et si vill Leit op enger Strooss.
@en There are a lot of people on a street.
@distractor-lu keng
@distractor-lu Autoen
@distractor-en no
@distractor-en cars

@sentence
@lu Et ass eng Foussgängerzon am Zentrum.
@en It is a pedestrian zone in the centre.
@distractor-lu Strooss
@distractor-lu Buttek
@distractor-en street
@distractor-en shop

@sentence
@lu D'Leit akafen an de Butteker.
@en The people are shopping in the shops.
@distractor-lu spillen
@distractor-lu Caféen
@distractor-en playing
@distractor-en pubs

@sentence
@lu Lénks ass e Chantier.
@en On the left there is a construction site.
@distractor-lu riets
@distractor-lu Gebai
@distractor-en right
@distractor-en building

@sentence
@lu D'Gebaier sinn héich an al.
@en The buildings are tall and old.
@distractor-lu kleng
@distractor-lu jonk
@distractor-en small
@distractor-en young

@sentence
@lu Viru de Butteker stinn e puer Clienten.
@en In front of the shops a few customers are standing.
@distractor-lu trëppelen
@distractor-lu Autoen
@distractor-en walking
@distractor-en cars

@sentence
@lu Et ass wierklech richteg, datt vill Leit an der Strooss sinn.
@en It is really true that a lot of people are in the street.
@distractor-lu falsch
@distractor-lu Butteker
@distractor-en false
@distractor-en shops

@sentence
@lu Hannen an der Strooss gesinn ech Beem an Autoen.
@en At the back of the street I see trees and cars.
@distractor-lu vir
@distractor-lu Butteker
@distractor-en front
@distractor-en shops

# --- Reusable frames (@fill) ---

# The place-noun frame. `an enger` stays in the frame, so the blank takes a
# feminine noun only — which rules out masculine `Zentrum` and `Buttek` on
# grammar alone (R4). Both distractors are also factually wrong about the photo,
# so the item does not rest on grammar by itself.
#
# `op dem Bild` is not padding: the library's bare `D'Persounen sinn …` puts the
# plural `-n` directly before `sinn`, where the Eifeler Regel drops it
# (`D'Persoune sinn`). Inserting a phrase that starts with a ZOAH letter keeps the
# `-n` intact, so the frame teaches the full form rather than one the learner
# would later have to un-learn. The n-drop audit flags the bare version.
@fill
@lu D'Persounen op dem Bild sinn an enger [Foussgängerzon] an der Stad.
@en The people in the picture are in a [pedestrian zone] in the city.
@distractor-lu Zentrum
@distractor-lu Buttek
@distractor-en centre
@distractor-en shop

# The inventory frame with two coordinated nouns. Blanking BOTH sides of `X a Y`
# is an R6 violation by construction — the two tiles would be interchangeable —
# so only the second side is a blank and `Butteker` stays fixed in the frame.
# `vill` plus the plural forces a plural tile, so the singular distractors are
# wrong on agreement as well as on the photo. The conjunction stays `a`, not
# `an`: `Caféen` starts with a consonant, and an `an` here would put an
# `-n`-final word directly before the blank and break R5.
@fill
@lu Lénks a riets ginn et vill Butteker a [Caféen].
@en On the left and on the right there are a lot of shops and [pubs].
@distractor-lu Bam
@distractor-lu Auto
@distractor-en tree
@distractor-en car

# The additive-listing frame. `Ausserdeem` is the connector that moves a
# candidate past a bare list, and it forces inversion (`gesinn ech`) — an order
# the learner reads here instead of assembling. Two blanks of different number
# and slot (plural noun, then singular after the fixed `e`), so they cannot swap.
@fill
@lu Ausserdeem gesinn ech [Stroosseluuchten] an e [Chantier].
@en Besides I see [street lamps] and a [construction site].
@distractor-lu Autoen
@distractor-lu Bam
@distractor-en cars
@distractor-en tree

# The background inventory — the mirror of the foreground frame in the other
# picture theme. `vill héich` is fixed, so the blank is a plural noun; both
# distractors are singular and fail agreement as well as the photo.
@fill
@lu Am Hannergrond ginn et vill héich [Gebaier].
@en In the background there are a lot of tall [buildings].
@distractor-lu Gebai
@distractor-lu Bam
@distractor-en building
@distractor-en tree

# The `mee`-contrast frame. `mee` is a connecting word (§3 of the grammar
# reference) and does NOT cause inversion, so `d'Leit akafen` keeps main-clause
# order on both sides — the frame teaches the connector without smuggling in a
# subordinate-clause rule it doesn't need. Two blanks in different clauses and
# different word classes (verb, then plural noun), so they cannot swap (R6).
@fill
@lu D'Leit [akafen] an de Butteker, mee kee kuckt an d'[Vitrinnen].
@en The people are [shopping] in the shops, but nobody looks at the [shop windows].
@distractor-lu spillen
@distractor-lu Autoen
@distractor-en playing
@distractor-en cars

# The `wann`-inversion frame — the harder half of the connector pattern the
# grammar reference calls out: a leading `wann`-clause forces the main clause to
# invert (`voll sinn` before its subject would be un-inverted). `Wann do vill`
# keeps `Wann` followed by the ZOAH-initial `do`, which is what the Eifeler
# Regel actually needs — `Wann vill` would drop the `-n` because `v` is outside
# the list. ONE blank on purpose: a `Leit`/`Clienten` pair in two slots would be
# an R6 violation — swapping the plural-noun tiles produces an equally
# grammatical, equally sensible sentence, just not the authored one. The blank
# is `voller`, a predicative adjective, which cannot swap with a noun at all.
@fill
@lu Wann do vill Leit an der Strooss sinn, sinn d'Butteker [voller] Clienten.
@en When there are a lot of people in the street, the shops are [full] of customers.
@distractor-lu eidel
@distractor-lu zou
@distractor-en empty
@distractor-en closed

# The `well`-justification frame — the reasoning shape every exam answer should
# reach for, not just the weather sub-lesson's. `well` sends the verb to the end
# (`sinn` last), so the learner reads that order instead of assembling it. Two
# blanks in different clauses (adjective, then noun after the fixed `vill`).
@fill
@lu D'Strooss gesäit [lieweg] aus, well vill [Leit] amgaang sinn ze akafen.
@en The street looks [lively], because a lot of [people] are in the middle of shopping.
@distractor-lu eidel
@distractor-lu Autoen
@distractor-en empty
@distractor-en cars

# The relative-clause frame — the shape a `@sentence` cannot safely ask the
# learner to assemble, since `deen … steet` is verb-final. The relative clause
# itself stays fixed and unblanked: `deen` is a single-`n` grammatical pronoun
# (the same class as `een`/`kann`, not the stem-final `-nn` nouns like `Mann`),
# so a blank anywhere near it or after the also-`-n`-final `Vitrinn` would be
# unfixable under the Eifeler Regel (R5) regardless of word order. Both blanks
# instead sit in the main clause, after `e` and `vu` (ZOAH-safe), and are
# different word classes with incompatible grammar — `e` takes a singular
# noun, `vu` takes the plural, so swapping breaks agreement (R6).
@fill
@lu Ech gesinn e [Grupp] vu [Leit], deen do viru enger Vitrinn steet.
@en I see a [group] of [people] who is standing there in front of a shop window.
@distractor-lu Chantier
@distractor-lu Auto
@distractor-en construction site
@distractor-en car

# The exemplifying-listing frame, mirrored from the funfair theme's `Zum
# Beispill` row but built on `munch` instead — "many a" is a fixed A1-safe
# quantifier that never declines, so it stays outside the blank on purpose,
# and the blanked noun after it is what forces agreement to do the work: a
# singular tile is the only grammatical fit, ruling out plural distractors.
# `Persoun déi` (not `Persoun mat`) keeps the first blank clear of the
# Eifeler Regel — `déi` starts with `d`, inside ZOAH — while still reading
# naturally into the second, comma-free clause.
@fill
@lu Zum Beispill gesinn ech munch [Persoun] déi mat engem [Kand] ass.
@en For example I see many a [person] who is with a [child].
@distractor-lu Autoen
@distractor-lu Butteker
@distractor-en cars
@distractor-en shops

# The `ausserdeem` + `wéi`-comparison frame — `wéi` here is the CONJ sense
# "as", not the interrogative "how", so the frame also disambiguates a
# homograph the grammar reference flags. `ausserdeem` forces inversion
# (`gesinn ech`), matching the additive frame already shipped; the `wéi`-clause
# stays main-clause order because `wéi` (as) does not send the verb to the end.
@fill
@lu Ausserdeem gesinn ech [Autoen], wéi [ëmmer] um Enn vun der Strooss.
@en Besides I see [cars], as [always] at the end of the street.
@distractor-lu Beem
@distractor-lu selten
@distractor-en trees
@distractor-en rarely

# The `zum Schluss` positional frame — the exam's "at the far end" shape, kept
# distinct from `am Hannergrond` (which the photo also uses) so the learner has
# both. `Do gesinn ech` keeps the verb early and followed by the ZOAH-initial
# `ech`, so the first blank only has to clear the comma before it, and the
# second clause's `mee kee … méi` omits repeating the verb (natural ellipsis
# under coordination) rather than putting a second verb right after a plural
# blank. `vill` forces a plural and `kee` forces a singular, so the two blanks
# cannot swap without breaking agreement (R6).
@fill
@lu Do gesinn ech nach vill [Autoen], mee kee [Chantier] méi.
@en There I still see a lot of [cars], but no [construction site] anymore.
@distractor-lu Beem
@distractor-lu Buttek
@distractor-en trees
@distractor-en shop

# The two-clause `mee`-contrast on the construction site, closing the loop on
# the source description's own `Lénks ass e Chantier` line with a second clause.
# `mee` stays a connector (no inversion), so `een do gesäit` keeps main-clause
# order; `een` sits directly before the ZOAH-initial `do`, which is what keeps
# it clear of the Eifeler Regel — an earlier draft had `kann`/`gesinn` right
# after `een` and both fail it. The blank after the fixed `keng` is a plural
# noun, which the negation and the photo both rule the distractors out of.
@fill
@lu Lénks ass e Chantier, mee een do gesäit keng [Butteker].
@en On the left there is a construction site, but there one sees no [shops].
@distractor-lu Autoen
@distractor-lu Beem
@distractor-en cars
@distractor-en trees
